  "query_preview": "..\nTonight-Thursday: A cold upper-level trough is carving \ninto the Northwest today. A leading shortwave passed through \nthe area today (Wednesday) and was responsible for the more \npersistent rain and mountain snow showers. This shortwave will \neject into BC this evening with the main, and reinforcing low \ndigging southward along 130W just off the WA Coast. This will \nallow the air mass to slowly moderate from southeast to \nnorthwest and shift the main threat for light showers back into \nthe Cascades and northern mountains. Given the warming aloft, \nshower intensities will be on the decline with minimal \nadditional snow accumulations in the mountains. Overnight \ntemperatures come with a moderate uncertainty. If the clouds \nbreak across Central and Northern WA, temperatures will be \ncapable of radiating well below freezing and does pose a small \nrisk for patchy black ice Thursday morning, especially for \nroadways within the higher terrain (~ 3000 ft). If the clouds \nhold, temperatures will only flirt with 32F with a lower risk \nfor ice development. A saturated boundary layer also suggest the\npotential for patchy fog and low stratus heading into Thursday \nmorning. \n\nFriday-Sunday: There is good agreement amongst the ensembles for \nthe upper-low to remain anchored off the WA Coast through early\nSaturday then fill and weaken while ejecting inland Saturday \nnight into Sunday. The position of this low off the coast does \nnot pose a large risk for widespread precipitation Friday into \nearly Saturday with highest chances for showers (30-40%) along \nthe Cascade Crest and 5-15% elsewhere. Snow levels will waver \nfrom 2500-3500 feet in the Cascades so motorists should be \nprepared for light snow showers at times if traveling across the\npasses. \n\nThe threat for precipitation will increase Saturday into Saturday \nnight and depart to the east on Sunday as the system tracks \ninland ushering a slow moving cold front from west to east. The \nmain threat will be across the eastern two-thirds of the \nforecast area. Rain chances vary from 40-60% across North Idaho \nand southeastern Washington Saturday evening/night and 15-25% \nfurther west for locations like Ritzville, Republic, Colville, \nand Davenport. Spokane sits around 25-30% with highest chances \nafter 11PM. Rainfall amounts look light and under a tenth of an \ninch through 11PM for most areas. After 11PM, rain will increase\nand become more steady across far southeastern WA and the lower\nIdaho Panhandle where there is a 20-40% chance for rainfall \namounts to exceed a quarter of an inch. Dry and cool conditions \nare expected across Central WA Saturday evening into Saturday \nnight. Winds Saturday evening will be light and generally under \n10 mph with evening temperatures in the mid to lower 40s.\n\nMonday-Wednesday: There is moderate confidence for any upper-level \nridge to build over the Northwest during this period. This will\npromote near to warmer than normal temperatures, light winds, \nand largely dry conditions. The caveat comes Tuesday with ~50% \nof the ensembles indicating a few different potential outcomes \nwhere a weak wave slips through the ridge delivering a chance \nfor light showers. By Wednesday, the ensembles re-align with \nnearly all outcomes showing the ridge back in place. All things \nconsidered, ridges in November can often lead to cool \ntemperatures due to the lack of mixing, especially following \nprecipitation. This will likely be the case for areas that pick \nup over an quarter of an inch of rain over the next few days. \nFog and stratus can also be slow to burn off each morning. At \nthis time, confidence is above normal for minimal weather \nimpacts during this period. /sb",
